Here's a PHP function to **paginate an array** (i.e., split it into multiple pages of results). This function takes the original array, the number of items per page (`$perPage`), and the current page number (`$page`), and returns a paginated array.

### ✅ Example Usage:

```php
function paginateArray($array, $perPage, $page = 1) {
    $totalItems = count($array);
    $currentPage = $page;
    $startIndex = ($currentPage - 1) * $perPage;
    $ endIndex = $startIndex + $perPage;

    $paginatedArray = array_slice($array, $startIndex, $perPage);

    // Calculate total pages
    $totalPages = ceil($totalItems / $perPage);

    // Optional: Add pagination links
    $paginationLinks = '';
    if ($totalPages > 1) {
        $paginationLinks = '<nav aria-label="Page navigation">';
        $paginationLinks .= '<ul class="pagination">';
        for ($i = 1; $i <= $totalPages; $i++) {
            $active = ($i == $currentPage) ? 'active' : '';
            $paginationLinks .= '<li class="page-item"><a href="#" class="page-link" aria-label="Go to page ' . $i . '">' . $i . '</a></li>';
        }
        $paginationLinks .= '</ul>';
        $paginationLinks .= '</nav>';
    }

    return [
        'data' => $paginatedArray,
        'totalPages' => $totalPages,
        'currentPage' => $currentPage,
        'paginationLinks' => $paginationLinks
    ];
}
